Call of Juarez Gunslinger is an FPS based on the Wild West, a title that was released as a digital download only, and priced as an indie game at launch, (15$ is an absolute steal) but with AAA production values.
After the underwhelming critical response to Call of Juarez The Cartel, developer Techland and publisher Ubisoft went back to the drawing board and revived a franchise many thought would never be relevant again, and for all intents and purposes, they managed to pull it off, Call of Juarez Gunslinger is a great Call of Juarez title, and an amazing shooter in it's own right.
The game has 3 main play modes, Story, Arcade and Duel. Story mode is the main one in the game, you play as Silas Greaves a bounty Hunter who has just arrived to a bar in Abilene, Texas and starts telling his life story to a few people at the local saloon, for a few free drinks. He tells about how he met Billy The Kid, Johnny Ringo, Sundance Kid, Butch Cassidy and other famous Wild West legends. The game's story and narration is told in a very different way than any other game, as you play the levels Silas himself is telling the story as he remembers it, and it creates a unique gaming experience in the sense that as you play a level, you hear him and the people around him, telling what happened and asking how the events took place. It is simple and it makes for a few twists to the story in the levels in real time, when Silas tells something that sounds almost impossible, it usually is, and he then tells the story "how it actually happened" making you play some levels in completely different ways. It is an interesting mechanic, and quite unique in how it presents the story to the player.
Arcade mode is all about getting through levels as fast as you can and rack up the most amount of points possible, it is incredibly fun to play and the combo system(which also exists in the story) for taking down enemies works amazingly well and is very rewarding.
Duel is a mode that allows you to play, or replay the Duels from the main story. It is what makes this game feel like it actually takes place in the Wild West, and it's also what made Call of Juarez unique to begin with. It is basically a 1 on 1 (sometimes 1 on 2) standout like you see in most Westerns worthy of that name, where the camera is focusing on your enemy from a behind your gun perspective and you need to focus and adjust the weapon draw speed to succeed. You will need fast reflexes to win these duels, and they are incredibly gratifying, and fun to play.
The visuals and audio in the game are quite amazing, even more so considering this is not a full budget AAA game. The game's engine is Techland's proprietary Chrome Engine 5, an engine used in Dead Island and Dead Island Riptide for example, and it uses a cel-shaded effect that makes the game look quite unique. It is similar in it's looks to the style Borderlands uses, although different enough to stand out on it's own. It is violent and bloody, and everything on screen looks great and plays fast. The music is one of the best parts of the game, it sets the mood quite well, and they are essencial to the experience in the way it works together with the visuals, the presentation and the overall feel of the game. Call of Juarez is in more ways than one, a true homage to the Wild West, to Spaghetti Westerns and to a time where the law and crime were seen in a completely different way than what it is today, and it delivers in spades.
The mechanics in the game are simple and work brilliantly. The shooting is a lot of fun and incredibly precise, making shooting the enemies fun and not frustrating. Concentration is an important part of the game's mechanics, allowing you to make time go slower and be even more precise, activated simply by the press of a button and it depletes and recharges over time, adding some strategy in how to best use it, and the last chance mode will give you the opportunity to dodge a bullet that will kill you, if you press the right direction, and it's context sensitive, meaning you will not always be able to use it. All these contribute to some dramatic and frantic gameplay moments, adding even more value to this title.
Do not let the low price tag fool you. Call of Juarez is an incredible game and has a lot of content for it's price. The story isn't that long, but it is fun and has just the right lenght. Arcade and Duel modes add even more content to the mix, and are unique and incredibly fun.
The Good
-Good story, great presentation and narration;
-Cutscenes presented in a comic/handrawn style gives the game a unique personality and visual style;
-Really great shooting mechanics;
-Tense Duels;
-Excellent visuals and audio;
-Real Wild West Lawmen and outlaws in the story;
-Highly replayable story mode;
-It's price;
The Bad
-Story mode may feel a little short to some;
-Levels are linear, for the most part which can be considered a downside for some players;
9/10

Its a class game. Shame the pad bug isnt fixed yet! Multiplayer version on the way I believe...
Anyone having problems with stuttering and or crashes due to memory leaks;
simply unplug any pads (i.e xbox one or ps4 pads) until the main menu game is loaded. Only then plug in and start straight into game.  Good luck

